#- Purpose :
#-           This README file indicates how to setup the Denali memory
#-           modeller envronment. These models are used for verification
#-           simulations.
#-
#- -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
#- Summary :
#- Denali memory models are third party memory models written by Denali, a
#- company specialising in memory models.
#- You can download a limited period licence from the Denali web site
#- www.denalisoft.com.
#-
#- --=========================================================================--

   Denali version used for PL172 is Denali Memory Model Version 3.000-0011

   All Denali models are used for the verification tests. Refer to the
   PL172 Design Manual (PL172_DDES_0000_A.doc) for details about test cases
   that uses Denali models.

Whilst SOMA files can be downloaded from the web page separately, the .spc and 
.dat files will be delivered as part of the PL172 delivery. These can be found in
the mpmc_pl172/verification/denali directory.

#- Below are the steps involved in using the DENALI memory models, including 
downloading them from the Denali website

1. Download Denali license
   You can download a limited period license from the Denali web-site.
   Log on to www.denalisoft.com for more information about getting a
   license.

2. Download SOMA files from the web
   Required 'Specification of Memory Architecture' (SOMA) files need to be
   downloaded from the web. It is available at the web-site www.ememory.com
   (Log on to www.ememory.com for more information about downloading
    SOMA files and their copyright terms)
   Downloaded SOMA files are kept in the verification/denali directory.

   .soma and .dat files of denali directory have the same name as that of the
   part name of the memory. 

3. Save the .soma as .spc and create VHDL/Verilog source
   a. Open the .soma file using memmaker
   b. Choose 'Save SOMA As' from the file menu
   c. Save as .spc file. Choose the name of the .spc as given in the table
      below

   Create VHDL/Verilog source
   VHDL or Verilog wrapper/source file for the memory models have to be
   generated using the PureView (memmaker) utility.
   Steps for creating the VHDL/Verilog source file :
   a. Open the SOMA using the PureView (memmaker)
      e.g. memmaker at27lv040a_15.soma &
   b. Select the 'Source' tab
   c. Select appropriate 'Simulation Environment' under the 'Options' menu
      e.g. If you use ModelTech Modelsim VHDL, select
              Model Technology Modelsim (Unix)
           If you use Cadence Verilog-XL simulator, select
              Cadence Verilog-XL
   d. Change the entity name according to the table below
   e. Make sure that the wrapper file is pointing to the corresponding
      .spc indeed.
   f. Save the source file in the mpmc_pl172/verification/vhdl/tbench/memmodels
      directory, in case of VHDL, and
      mpmc_pl172/verification/verilog/tbench/memmodels directory, in case of
      Verilog, with the same name as entity and with a .vhd or .v extension by
      selecting 'Save Source' option under the File menu.
      e.g. Save source as at27lv040a_15.vhd in
           mpmc_pl172/verification/vhdl/tbench directory.
           (In case of Verilog source files, keep it in
            mpmc_pl172/verification/verilog/tbench directory)
   g. Close memmaker

   Repeat step 3 for all models.

4. Set the DENALIRC environment variable
   Set the DENALIRC environment variable to denali directory. All the user
   preferences for Denali simulations are specified in the .denalirc file
   in the denali directory.
   e.g. setenv DENALIRC ~/armmpmc/mpmc_pl172/verification/denali

5. Running Denali Memory Model based Simulation with Modelsim VHDL
   Prior to running Denali memory model based simulation with Modelsim VHDL,
   set the DENALI and license path variable to proper values.

6. Running Denali Memory Model based Simulation with Modelsim Verilog
   Prior to running Denali memory model based simulation with Modelsim Verilog,
   a. Set the DENALI and license path variable to proper values.
   b. Add the following line :
      Veriuser = $DENALI/mtipli.so
      in the [vsim] section of the local modelsim.ini from where you invoke
      vsim [Refer to README_Denali in mpmc_pl172/verification/verilog]

7. Running Denali Memory Model based Simulation with LDV Verilog-XL
   For running Denali Memory model based simulations with LDV Verilog-XL, a
   customised local Verilog-XL executable needs to be created. This custom
   Verilog-XL executable will contain the required PLI information. Please
   refer to the following readme help files for the creation of the Verilog-XL
   executable :
   a. mpmc_pl172/verification/verilog/README_Denali
   b. $DENALI/verilog/README.verilog

8. Running Denali Memory Model based Simulation with any other supported
   Simulator
   Refer to the corresponding README help file at $DENALI/verilog directory

